import TDA.Reeb.Components
import TDA.Reeb.ComponentsDiscrete
import Mathlib.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.Connectivity.Connected
import Mathlib.Topology.Subpath
import Mathlib.Topology.UnitInterval

/-!
# Realizations of simple graphs and their path components

For `V : Type u` and `G : SimpleGraph V`, the cells are `Cells G = V ⊕ Σ d : G.Dart, unitInterval`:
the vertex summand carries the discrete topology `⊥`, whatever instance `V` may have, and the edge
summand the sigma topology of copies of the unit interval. `Realization G` is the quotient `Quot` of
the cells by the relation `CellRel G` generated by `(d, t) ~ (d.symm, σ t)` and `(d, 0) ~ d.fst`,
with the quotient topology; two cells are identified exactly when the generated equivalence relates
them (`quotMk_eq_quotMk_iff`). The other endpoint equation `(d, 1) ~ d.snd` follows by reversal
(`edgePoint_one`). No finiteness, local finiteness, nonemptiness, decidability, separation or
vertex-topology hypothesis is used.

## Universal property

Vertex data `fv : V → Y` and edge data `fe : G.Dart → unitInterval → Y` satisfying the reversal and
start equations descend to `lift`, which is continuous when every edge map is (`continuous_lift`)
and unique among all functions (`lift_unique`). `continuousLift` is the bundled form for continuous
edge data `G.Dart → C(unitInterval, Y)`, unique among continuous maps (`continuousLift_unique`,
`existsUnique_continuousLift`). Distinct vertices stay distinct (`vertex_injective`), by a set-level
retraction; no continuous retraction to the vertices is asserted.

## Paths and maps

`edgePath d` runs from `vertex G d.fst` to `vertex G d.snd`. For `s t : unitInterval`,
`edgeSegment G d s t` is the subpath from `edgePoint G d s` to `edgePoint G d t`, with parameter
`Set.Icc.convexComb s t r` (`edgeSegment_apply`, `edgeSegment_zero`, `edgeSegment_one`).
`walkPath` concatenates the edge paths of a walk, and every edge point is joined to the first
endpoint of its dart. A graph homomorphism
`φ : G →g H` induces `realizationMap φ : C(Realization G, Realization H)` with
`vertex v ↦ vertex (φ v)` and `edgePoint d t ↦ edgePoint (φ.mapDart d) t`, and with identity and
composition laws as equalities of continuous maps; the vertex types may live in independent
universes. A graph homomorphism preserves adjacency, so edges go to edges and no edge is collapsed
to a vertex; nonadjacent vertices and distinct edges may be identified.

## Path components

`componentsEquiv G : ZerothHomotopy (Realization G) ≃ G.ConnectedComponent` sends the class of a
vertex to its graph component and the class of an edge point to the component of the first endpoint
of its dart; its inverse sends a graph component to the class of a representing vertex. It is
natural for the path-component map `TDA.Reeb.componentMap` and mathlib's
`SimpleGraph.ConnectedComponent.map` (`componentsEquiv_componentMap`). A graph is connected exactly
when its realization is path connected (`connected_iff_pathConnectedSpace`); both notions include
nonemptiness.

The short names `Realization`, `instTopologicalSpaceRealization`, `quotMk`, `quotMk_surjective`,
`isQuotientMap_quotMk`, `continuous_quotMk`, `lift`, `lift_unique` and `hom_ext` also occur in
`TDA.Reeb.CylinderPresentation`, `lift`, `lift_unique`, `hom_ext` and `ind` in
`TDA.Reeb.ReebQuotient`, and `edgePoint` and `continuous_edgePoint` in `TDA.Reeb.RGraph`; qualify
them when opening more than one of these namespaces.

